# .is-template — marker file

This repository **IS the template** (the repo people copy from), not a project
scaffolded from it. Nothing else.

What it changes:

- `AGENTS.md` rule 7: shared docs (`AGENTS.md`, `docs/progress.md`,
  `docs/architecture.md`, `docs/adr/README.md`) may be edited directly on a
  feature branch here, with the human's approval. Everywhere else, branches only
  write their `docs/changes/<branch>.md` fragment.
- `.github/workflows/guard.yml`: the shared-docs check reports a notice and passes
  instead of failing the PR.

Delete it the moment you scaffold a project from this template, together with the
`TODO(project)` sweep:

    git grep -n "TODO(project)"
    rm .is-template        # PowerShell: Remove-Item .is-template

After deletion the shared-docs rule applies in full and `guard.yml` starts
enforcing it on every pull request.

This file survives renames and forks on purpose — it answers "what role does this
repo play?", which the repository name cannot.
